{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/finite_spectral_shadows.tex","new_string":"At $m=20$ the rate is flat in $c$ to $\\pm0.6\\%$. The structural reason is\nexact: for $m\\ge c$ the endpoint obstruction of \\cref{prop:endpoint}\n\\emph{evaporates}.\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Complete-kernel formula]\\label{prop:completekernel}\nLet $m\\ge c$. Then $3^m\\equiv0\\pmod{3^c}$, so the $m$-step endpoint\n$S_w(a)\\equiv C_w2^{-B_m(w)}\\pmod{3^c}$ is determined by the word alone,\nindependently of the source $a$. Consequently the kernel of $T^m$ factorizes\nas\n\\[\nK_m\\bigl((x,\\sigma),(a,\\tau)\\bigr)\n=\\sum_{\\substack{w:\\;C_w2^{-B_m(w)}\\equiv x\\ (3^c)\\\\ B_m(w)\\equiv\\sigma-\\tau\\ (2)}}\np_s(w)\\,\\Psi_w(a),\n\\qquad\n\\Psi_w(a)=\\prod_{i=0}^{m-1}\\chi\\bigl(x_i(a,w)\\bigr),\n\\]\nand the entries of $H_m=(T^m)^*T^m$ are \\emph{complete word-pair sums}:\n\\[\nH_m\\bigl((a,\\tau),(a',\\tau')\\bigr)\n=\\sum_{\\substack{(w,w'):\\;C_w2^{-B_m(w)}\\equiv C_{w'}2^{-B_m(w')}\\ (3^c)\\\\\nB_m(w)\\equiv B_m(w')+\\tau'-\\tau\\ (2)}}\np_s(w)\\,p_s(w')\\,\\overline{\\Psi_w(a)}\\,\\Psi_{w'}(a').\n\\]\nThe common-endpoint constraint selects word pairs only---no source coset\nappears---so the incomplete-coset obstruction of \\cref{prop:endpoint} is\nstructurally absent in this regime, and the Schur test becomes available.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\paragraph{Row-sum audit (Schur sharpness).} For the unweighted Schur bound\n$\\lVert T^m\\rVert^2\\le R_m:=\\sup_{\\xi}\\sum_{\\xi'}\\lvert H_m(\\xi,\\xi')\\rvert$\n(states $\\xi=(a,\\tau)$), at $s=0$ and $m=c,\\,c+2,\\,2c$:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc|ccc|ccc}\n& \\multicolumn{3}{c|}{c=5} & \\multicolumn{3}{c|}{c=6}\n& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{c=7}\\\\\nm & 5 & 7 & 10 & 6 & 8 & 12 & 7 & 9 & 14\\\\\\hline\n\\sigma_1(T^m)^{1/m} & 0.9231 & 0.8364 & 0.7706\n                    & 0.9096 & 0.8373 & 0.7655\n                    & 0.9040 & 0.8363 & 0.7443\\\\\nR_m^{1/(2m)}        & 0.9547 & 0.8600 & 0.7849\n                    & 0.9350 & 0.8567 & 0.7823\n                    & 0.9280 & 0.8620 & 0.7676\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe Schur bound tracks $\\sigma_1^{1/m}$ within $3\\%$ at every $(c,m)$, and\nsingular-vector weights move it by $\\le1\\%$ in either direction: the\n\\emph{unweighted} row sum is essentially sharp, so the Schur norm is the\ncorrect vehicle and no weighted refinement is forced. At $m=2c$ the bound\nitself descends in $c$ ($0.7849,\\,0.7823,\\,0.7676$). The effective constant\n$A_m(c)=R_m/\\Delta_s^m$ of the anticipated shape $R_m\\le A_m\\Delta_s^m$\nsatisfies $A_m^{1/(2m)}=1.61,\\,1.49,\\,1.33$ at $c=7$, $m=7,9,14$\n(descending): the row-multiplicity growth rate is $\\approx1.8^m$, comfortably\nbelow the critical rate $3^m$ at which the bound\n$\\lVert T^m\\rVert^{1/m}\\le A_m^{1/(2m)}\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}$ becomes vacuous at\n$s=0$. The remaining finite-side statement is therefore:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Large-$m$ complete-kernel Schur contraction]\\label{conj:schur}\nFix $s=0$ and the unit-mass normalization $T=W_s^{-1}M_{\\chi,c,s}$. There\nexist $\\alpha\\ge1$, $\\delta>0$ and $c_1$ such that for all $c\\ge c_1$, with\n$m=\\lfloor\\alpha c\\rfloor$ and uniformly over primitive non-coboundary $\\chi$\nmodulo $3^c$,\n\\[\n\\lVert T_{\\chi,c,0}^{\\,m}\\rVert_{2\\to2}\\le(1-\\delta)^m,\n\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad\nR_m\\le(1-\\delta)^{2m}.\n\\]\nBy the audit it suffices to prove $A_m(c)\\le C^m$ with $C<3$: the word-pair\nbookkeeping must beat a row-multiplicity growth rate of $3^m$ in the complete\nsums of \\cref{prop:completekernel}. The observed rate $\\approx0.70$ at $m=2c$\nis the empirical scale of $1-\\delta$ and is not part of the statement; the\nuniform-$s$ extension ($\\Delta_s$ in place of $\\Delta_0$, $s\\in[0,1]$) is a\nsubsequent target. This is a fixed-power operator-norm statement---outside\nthe scope of the trace ceiling (\\cref{rem:sixth})---and it implies\n$\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1$ directly. The numerical singular-value data do not yet\nprove the uniform gap; they identify the correct non-trace norm in which the\nalready-proved vanishing and flattening lemmas\n(\\cref{lem:vanish,lem:coset,lem:flat}) should be assembled, via a row-sum\ndecomposition of the word pairs into nonstationary, thin, prefix-locked\nstationary, and degenerate-minimum classes---the stationary directions being\nspectrally inert by \\cref{thm:eject}.\n\\end{conjecture}","old_string":"At $m=20$ the rate is flat in $c$ to $\\pm0.6\\%$. The structural reason is\nidentifiable: for $m\\ge c$ the endpoint obstruction of \\cref{prop:endpoint}\n\\emph{evaporates}---the entry-constraint cosets $a\\in a_0+3^{c-m}\\Z$ are all of\n$U_{3^c}$, so the matrix entries of $(T^m)^*T^m$ become \\emph{complete} sums to\nwhich the exact vanishing and flattening machinery applies entrywise, and the\nSchur test becomes available. The remaining finite-side statement is\ntherefore:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Large-$m$ Schur estimate]\\label{conj:schur}\nThere are $m\\asymp c$ and $\\delta>0$ such that, uniformly over primitive\nnon-coboundary $\\chi$,\n$\\lVert T_{\\chi,c,s}^{\\,m}\\rVert\\le(1-\\delta)^m$. Equivalently, the complete\nword-pair sums forming the entries of $(T^m)^*T^m$ in the regime $m\\ge c$\nsatisfy entrywise bounds summable under a weighted Schur test. This is a\nfixed-power operator-norm statement---an eigenvector-aware object outside the\nscope of the trace ceiling (\\cref{rem:sixth})---and it implies\n$\\sup_c\\gamma_c(s)<1$ directly, with the measured rate $\\approx0.70$ at $s=0$\nbounding the achievable $\\delta$.\n\\end{conjecture}","replace_all":false}